  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research an orientation-adaptive algorithm: pair boundaries along the smaller matrix dimension, extend each pair while accumulating values along the larger dimension, then count 1D subarrays summing to k with prefix frequencies. This reduces work from the current O(M*N^2) orientation to O(min(M,N)^2*max(M,N)); retain signed 64-bit prefix sums and answer counts, and verify correctness with negative values as well as positive values.",
        "B": "Research parallelizing the independent strip-pair computations, initially keeping the existing column-pair formulation distinct from A’s algorithm change. Give each worker private scratch storage, frequency state, and answer accumulation; avoid per-prefix synchronization or a shared hash map. On the 4-core ARM64 target, compare parallel and sequential paths, including whether a workload-based sequential fallback avoids overhead for small workloads.",
        "C": "Research the frequency-counting kernel used for each strip: compare the existing unordered_map with a reserved map and a reusable open-addressed table using 64-bit keys/counts and safe collision handling. Bound table capacity from the number of prefixes in a strip and reset it correctly between strips (and independently per worker if combined with B). Preserve exact prefix-frequency counting; do not rely on input-value or test-case assumptions.",
        "G": "Evaluate A, B, and C outputs independently, then integrate only variants supported by measurements. Measure the integrated candidates on the actual target and correctness suite: algorithmic orientation, parallelism, and a faster table may interact, so compare promising combinations rather than assuming gains add. Keep any workload-based dispatch generic, use no more than the target’s four cores, and retain the fastest verified implementation without weakening correctness for any valid input.",
        "analysis": "The supplied baseline is correct on 4 cases and averages 0.2484148 seconds across three runs (score 0.5); its source pairs columns, scans rows, and uses an unordered_map in the O(M*N^2) hot loop. The problem permits negative values and dimensions below 1000, so prefix-frequency counting is appropriate while sliding-window assumptions are not; signed 64-bit arithmetic is prudent. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU."
      },
      "request_sha256": "3c5fd454d70773d8b49b798cfef75b024e496b54f027488d972306577aef9ae7",
