    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Using the 0.02708-second leader as the baseline, investigate the input and matrix layout: can tall matrices be stored in the scan-friendly orientation as they are read, or transposed more efficiently, while keeping paired boundaries on the shorter dimension and scans contiguous? Measure full-program runtime; earlier transpose results were slower than the current leader and are not directly comparable.",
        "B": "Investigate parallel work distribution on the winning rolling-band algorithm. Its outer band starts have unequal work, roughly proportional to (p−start)·q; compare dynamic and guided scheduling with weighted chunks or other balanced assignments, retaining per-thread scratch and a 64-bit answer reduction. Measure small and skinny inputs too, and keep a generic sequential fallback if parallel overhead outweighs the work.",
        "C": "Investigate reducing frequency-table costs while preserving the winning algorithm. From the official bounds, a scanned prefix has magnitude at most 999·999·1000 = 998,001,000; prefix−k remains within signed 32-bit range, and a key occurs at most 1,000 times per scan. Benchmark signed 32-bit keys and narrow per-key counters with a proven-safe hash table, while keeping intermediate checks and the total answer 64-bit. Size the table for a safe load factor and handle generation wraparound; do not use a direct-address table for this wide key range.",
        "G": "Integrate candidate programs from A, B, and C, starting from the current measured leader (mean 0.0270799 s; commit 1fd4abfb250cf25218001a6de1353d669e4b3c07). Check correctness for every candidate and measure complete-program runtime on the unchanged target and harness. Test combinations rather than assuming their gains add; retain only a measured, correct improvement. The baseline mean is 0.2484148 s, while prior alternatives measured 0.04187–0.09454 s, so do not assume a new integration is faster than its inputs.",
        "analysis": "The supplied source uses O(m·n²) band scans with an unordered_map for prefix frequencies; the baseline mean is 0.2484148 s. The best observed program, at 0.0270799 s (about 9.17× the baseline), pairs boundaries along the smaller dimension, accumulates rolling bands, uses a generation-tagged open-addressed frequency table, and dynamically parallelizes independent band starts. Other measured variants were slower, so use that leader as the comparison point and test any integration empirically. Preserve exact results for all legal inputs; the maximum answer needs 64-bit storage."
      },
